    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d219">

                <pb facs="0504" n="482" sameAs="frus:frus1950v05/pg482"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">Secretary’s Daily Meetings: Lot 58 D 609: March
                        1950<anchor n="1" xml:id="d219-f1"/>
                </note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">Memorandum of Conversation, by the Director of the
                        Executive Secretariat</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">McWilliams</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">secret</hi>
                    </seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">[<placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>,] <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1950-03-01">March 1, 1950</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p rendition="#flush">Subject: Summary of Daily Meeting with the Secretary</p>
                <table cols="3" rows="6">
                    <row role="label">
                        <cell rendition="#top">Participants:</cell>
                        <cell>The Secretary</cell>
                        <cell>Mr. Nitze<anchor n="2" xml:id="d219-f2"/>
                        </cell>
                    </row>
                    <row>
                        <cell/>
                        <cell>Mr. Webb<anchor n="3" xml:id="d219-f3"/>
                        </cell>
                        <cell>Mr. Merchant<anchor n="4" xml:id="d219-f4"/>
                        </cell>
                    </row>
                    <row>
                        <cell/>
                        <cell>Mr. Rusk</cell>
                        <cell>Mr. Humelsine<anchor n="5" xml:id="d219-f5"/>
                        </cell>
                    </row>
                    <row>
                        <cell/>
                        <cell>Mr. Barrett<anchor n="6" xml:id="d219-f6"/>
                        </cell>
                        <cell>Mr. Hare<anchor n="7" xml:id="d219-f7"/>
                        </cell>
                    </row>
                    <row>
                        <cell/>
                        <cell>Mr. McFall<anchor n="8" xml:id="d219-f8"/>
                        </cell>
                        <cell>Mr. Battle<anchor n="9" xml:id="d219-f9"/>
                        </cell>
                    </row>
                    <row>
                        <cell/>
                        <cell>Mr. Perkins<anchor n="10" xml:id="d219-f10"/>
                        </cell>
                        <cell>Mr. McWilliams<anchor n="11" xml:id="d219-f11"/>
                        </cell>
                    </row>
                </table>
                <p>Item 1. <hi rendition="#italic">Iran</hi> [Action: Mr. Reber<anchor n="12"
                        xml:id="d219-f12"/>]</p>
                <p>The Secretary asked for an explanation of the situation in Iran as reported in
                    Telegram 330.<anchor n="13" xml:id="d219-f13"/> Mr. Hare said that undoubtedly
                    the situation was not good but that we periodically receive cries of “wolf” from
                    that area and we are never quite sure when there is a genuine <pb facs="0505"
                        n="483" xml:id="pg483"/> cry. In this case he explained the cries of “wolf”
                    have been received before the facts of the situation.</p>
                <p>The Secretary asked if this was not a case of corrupt and incompetent government
                    which no matter how much equipment or money we put into it it would be doomed to
                    fail. Mr. Hare said this was somewhat the case and that we think the seven year
                    plan gives the best promise for helping Iran. He explained our assistance in
                    this plan although it is to be carried out and financed by the Iranian
                    government.</p>
                <p>Mr. Rusk said he thought Mrs. Pinchot<anchor n="14" xml:id="d219-f14"/> might
                    help on this.</p>
                <p>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">Note to Mr. Reber:</hi> I asked Mr. Peurifoy<anchor
                        n="15" xml:id="d219-f15"/> to see Mrs. Pinchot and see about giving her a
                    job. I think we had better check up on this to see what, if anything, has been
                        done.<anchor n="16" xml:id="d219-f16"/>
                </p>
                <p>[Here follows summary of discussion on items other than Iran.]</p>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d219-f1" n="1" xml:id="d219-f1-content">
                <p>Lot 58 D 609 is a chronological collection of the records of the Secretary of
                    State’s daily meetings with top Department of State officials for the years
                    1949–1952, as maintained by the special Assistant to the Secretary of State.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d219-f2" n="2" xml:id="d219-f2-content">
                <p>Paul H. Nitze, Director of the Policy Planning Council, Department of State.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d219-f3" n="3" xml:id="d219-f3-content">
                <p>James E. Webb, Under Secretary of State.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d219-f4" n="4" xml:id="d219-f4-content">
                <p>Livingston T. Merchant, Deputy Assistant Secretary of State for Far Eastern
                    Affairs.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d219-f5" n="5" xml:id="d219-f5-content">
                <p>Carlisle H. Humelsine, Deputy Assistant Secretary of State for
                    Administration.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d219-f6" n="6" xml:id="d219-f6-content">
                <p>Edward W. Barrett, Assistant Secretary of State for Public Affairs.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d219-f7" n="7" xml:id="d219-f7-content">
                <p>Raymond A. Hare, Deputy Assistant Secretary of State for Near Eastern, South
                    Asian, and African Affairs.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d219-f8" n="8" xml:id="d219-f8-content">
                <p>Jack K. McFall, Assistant Secretary of State for Congressional Relations.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d219-f9" n="9" xml:id="d219-f9-content">
                <p>Lucius D. Battle, Special Assistant to the Secretary of State.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d219-f10" n="10" xml:id="d219-f10-content">
                <p>George W. Perkins, Assistant Secretary of State for European Affairs.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d219-f11" n="11" xml:id="d219-f11-content">
                <p>William J. McWilliams, Director of the Executive Secretariat, Department of
                    State.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d219-f12" n="12" xml:id="d219-f12-content">
                <p>James Q. Reber, Chief of the Committee Secretariat Staff of the Executive
                    Secretariat, Department of State. Brackets in the source text.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d219-f13" n="13" xml:id="d219-f13-content">
                <p>February 27, from Tehran, <hi rendition="#italic">supra</hi>.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d219-f14" n="14" xml:id="d219-f14-content">
                <p>The Department offered Cornelia Bryce Pinchot, widow of Gifford Pinchot, former
                    Governor of Pennsylvania, an assignment as a Foreign Service reserve officer at
                    the Embassy in Iran for three or four months to develop contacts with Iranian
                    labor and peasant groups (letter, March 7, by Mr. Hare to Mrs. Pinchot;
                    123.1/1–1150). The editors have not found records in Department of State files
                    to indicate she accepted and carried out this assignment.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d219-f15" n="15" xml:id="d219-f15-content">
                <p>John E. Peurifoy, Deputy Under Secretary of State for Administration.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d219-f16" n="16" xml:id="d219-f16-content">
                <p>This paper bears the notation: “Assignment made Mar. 2. J[ames] Q. R[eber]. Hare
                    asked by A to follow up.”</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
